Practical 2: Basic Static Analysis of Windows Binaries on Linux
Aim

To perform basic static analysis of Windows executable files using Linux-based tools without executing the binary.

Objectives
To identify the type and structure of a Windows executable
To extract readable and hidden strings from the binary
To analyze metadata and embedded content
To study the internal structure using disassembly tools
To understand indicators of suspicious behavior in binaries
Procedure (Step-by-Step on Linux)
Step 1: Create a test executable (safe sample)

A sample Windows executable was created to simulate a suspicious file.

Create a C file:
nano fake_malware.c
Add the following code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Connecting to http://malicious-site.com\n");
    printf("Executing cmd.exe\n");
    printf("Downloading payload...\n");
    return 0;
}
Install MinGW compiler:
sudo apt install mingw-w64
Compile into Windows executable:
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc fake_malware.c -o sample.exe
Step 2: Identify file type
file sample.exe
Confirms the file is a Windows Portable Executable (PE).
Step 3: Extract strings from binary
strings sample.exe | head

To detect suspicious content:

strings sample.exe | grep -i "http"
strings sample.exe | grep -i "cmd"
Step 4: Analyze metadata using ExifTool
exiftool sample.exe
Step 5: Detect embedded data using Binwalk
binwalk sample.exe
Step 6: Inspect binary structure using Objdump
objdump -x sample.exe | head

Optional:

objdump -d sample.exe | head
Outcome

A safe sample executable was created and successfully analyzed using static analysis tools. Important information such as file type, strings, metadata, and structure was extracted without executing the file.

Result

The generated executable was identified as a Windows PE file. Static analysis tools (strings, exiftool, binwalk, objdump) successfully revealed embedded strings and structural details, demonstrating how suspicious indicators can be identified without running the program.
